WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:27.680
RED FLAGS:
November 2, 2022,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.680 --> 00:00:33.640
a sus document would be released: the balance&nbsp;
sheet of a mysterious trading firm called Alameda&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.640 --> 00:00:40.720
research. Alameda research was the sister firm&nbsp;
of the famed cryptocurrency empire that was FTX,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:46.320
and at first glance, this document was actually&nbsp;
reassuring. It showed that Alameda research had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:53.240
$14.6 billion worth of assets, a testament to&nbsp;
the pure size and scale of the FTX empire. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.240 --> 00:01:00.160
on closer examination, there were a few concerning&nbsp;
line items, specifically Alameda’s unusually large&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:09.680
stake in FTT, an altcoin created by FTX. Not only&nbsp;
did FTT account for $3.66 billion but it was the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.680 --> 00:01:14.840
single largest asset on their balance sheet. And&nbsp;
the 3rd largest asset on their balance sheet was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:14.840 --> 00:01:22.640
$2.16 billion worth of collateral on FTT. This&nbsp;
made it clear that Alameda Research and FTX were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.640 --> 00:01:29.920
far more connected that anyone initially expected&nbsp;
but this in itself didn’t implicate FTX. All we&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.920 --> 00:01:36.240
knew was that FTX was likely using their sister&nbsp;
company to pump up the price their own token. But&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:42.720
all of this would change just 4 days later when&nbsp;
the CEO of Binance, CZ, announced that they would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.720 --> 00:01:48.680
be liquidating their entire stake in FTT due&nbsp;
to recent revelations. Just 15 minutes later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.680 --> 00:01:53.560
Alameda research who was scrambling would&nbsp;
announce that they would buy Binance’s entire&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.560 --> 00:02:00.320
stake in a behind the scenes deal to soften the&nbsp;
sale’s impact on the market price of FTT. Now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:06.120
this was a red flag. Why was Alameda&nbsp;
so scared of an open market sale? How&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.120 --> 00:02:13.440
dependent were they really on FTT? We didn’t get&nbsp;
the answer to this immediately but we would hear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:20.196
some more unexepcted news. On November 8, 2022,&nbsp;
Binance announced that they were entering a non&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.196 --> 00:02:26.640
binding contract to buyout FTX to help them with&nbsp;
their “liquidity crunch”. Now, this was really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:33.600
concerning. Up until this point, FTX was the one&nbsp;
that was bailing out other exchanges and now FTX&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:39.480
themselves needed a bailout? This couldn’t be&nbsp;
good news and as expected, just one day later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:44.160
Bloomberg announced that US regulators were&nbsp;
investigating FTX for mishandling customers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:59.760
funds. This was the beginning of the end for Sam&nbsp;
Bankman Fried and FTX. Here’s what happened next.

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:03.120
ARRESTED:
Once regulators got involved, Sam would throw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:03.120 --> 00:03:09.080
in the towel almost immediately. He would announce&nbsp;
that Alameda research was shutting down, and just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.080 --> 00:03:16.160
one day after that, FTX would file for bankruptcy.&nbsp;
In retrospect, it’s obvious why Sam decided to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:22.160
this but at the time, this move was for more&nbsp;
concerning. This was the first indication from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.160 --> 00:03:29.080
Sam himself that there was more going on that what&nbsp;
met the eye. If all FTX needed was just funding,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.080 --> 00:03:34.080
there was no need to throw in the towel so early&nbsp;
as they may have still been able to get funding&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:40.240
given their size and reputation. But, if there was&nbsp;
something more sinister going on, it was better&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.240 --> 00:03:46.400
that Sam came clean sooner rather than later given&nbsp;
that this would no longer be about saving FTX,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:52.280
it would be about saving himself. And just as&nbsp;
people worried, FTX users’ worst fears would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.280 --> 00:03:59.320
come to fruition just two days later. $1 billion&nbsp;
worth of client funds were missing. And just when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:06.280
you thought things couldn’t get worse, FTX would&nbsp;
get hacked leading to $477 million moving off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:13.000
the exchange. The walls were quickly closing in on&nbsp;
Sam and he knew it. On November 15, a class action&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.000 --> 00:04:18.360
lawsuit would be filed against FTX and all of its&nbsp;
celebrity influencers including the likes of Kevin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:23.840
O’Leary, Shaquille O’Neal, and Stephen Curry.&nbsp;
Congress would also soon get involved announcing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:29.240
that they would be holding hearings regarding the&nbsp;
FTX implosion in December. But, despite all of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:35.360
this, Sam would play it off rather cool at least&nbsp;
publicly. While he admitted that he had screwed up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:41.840
big time, his biggest regret was actually having&nbsp;
filed for bankruptcy. He went on to argue that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.840 --> 00:04:46.720
may have been able to raise the required funds and&nbsp;
that this entire crisis could have been avoided if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.720 --> 00:04:52.360
he hadn’t filed for bankruptcy. But, while there&nbsp;
may have be some reality in which this may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:52.360 --> 00:05:00.280
worked, this isn’t exactly making customers whole.&nbsp;
Rather, this would just extend the deception. Not&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.280 --> 00:05:05.320
only would customers be getting fooled but so&nbsp;
would investors who would all be covering for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:05.320 --> 00:05:10.400
what at best was gross negligence and ignorance&nbsp;
and what at worst was straight up financial fraud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.400 --> 00:05:16.680
on Sam’s side. And make no mistake, those are the&nbsp;
only two options as it’s highly unlikely that this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:23.560
was actually just an honest mistake. You see, even&nbsp;
before the whole FTT token thing, Alameda research&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:23.560 --> 00:05:31.800
had lost $3.7 billion between 2019 and 2021, so&nbsp;
the core of FTX was never really as strong as Sam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:38.240
portrayed and people were starting to realize. And&nbsp;
in a last ditch effort to save face, Sam would try&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.240 --> 00:05:43.720
pulling off a massive publicity stunt. Starting&nbsp;
at the November, Sam would constantly appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.720 --> 00:05:48.840
on financial news networks, crypto podcasts,&nbsp;
conferences, and community townhalls. His goal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.840 --> 00:05:55.520
with all of this was crystal clear: he wanted to&nbsp;
paint his own narrative. Yes, he had had messed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.520 --> 00:06:01.560
up with FTX but it wasn’t because of malicious&nbsp;
intent or deliberate wrongdoing. It was because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:07.920
he just didn’t know. And hey maybe that is his&nbsp;
truth, but the reality for everyone else doesn’t&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:13.400
get any better whether he did it on purpose or&nbsp;
not. And soon enough, he too would understand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:19.040
this reality as his Congree hearing was rapidly&nbsp;
approaching on December 13. But this hearing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:19.040 --> 00:06:22.800
would never take place as on December 12, 2022,&nbsp;
Sam Bankman would be arrested in the Bahamas.

00:06:23.440 --> 00:06:35.240
THE CARDS FALL:
Sam would continue holding strong&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:35.240 --> 00:06:42.600
to his story but the same could not be said about&nbsp;
his business partners or should I say accomplices.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:48.400
Less than 10 days after Sam’s arrest, FTX co&nbsp;
founder, Gary Wang, and Alameda Reserach co-CEO,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:54.600
Caroline Ellison would both plead guilty and&nbsp;
agree to cooperate with prosecutors. Wang pled&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.600 --> 00:06:59.880
guilty to conspiracy to commit wire fraud, wire&nbsp;
fraud, conspiracy to commit commodities fraud&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:59.880 --> 00:07:05.280
and conspiracy to commit securities fraud. And&nbsp;
Ellison pled guilty to two counts of wire fraud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.280 --> 00:07:09.120
two counts of conspiracy to commit wire&nbsp;
fraud, conspiracy to commit commodities fraud,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:13.920
conspiracy to commit securities fraud and&nbsp;
conspiracy to commit money laundering. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:13.920 --> 00:07:19.440
now that these two had turned on Sam, we would see&nbsp;
a lot more damning evidence come out about just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:25.360
how involved Sam may have really been. According&nbsp;
to Gary Wang, Sam had directly instructed him to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.360 --> 00:07:32.320
create a secret backdoor to siphon client funds&nbsp;
from FTX into Alameda research. Gary went onto&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.320 --> 00:07:37.840
accomplish this by hiding a single line of code&nbsp;
in a sea of millions giving Alameda a total credit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:37.840 --> 00:07:46.120
line of wait for it $65 billion. Alameda allegedly&nbsp;
used some of this money to buy planes, houses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.120 --> 00:07:52.200
throw parties, and make political donations.&nbsp;
Apparantly, FTX owned a total of a quarter billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:58.000
dollars worth Bahamin real estate and spent as&nbsp;
much as $6.9 million on food and entertainment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.000 --> 00:08:04.320
within a matter of 9 months. Officially speaking,&nbsp;
FTX wasn’t stealing any clients funds, they were&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.320 --> 00:08:23.400
just “borrowing it” without permission. It looks&nbsp;
like Sam took Mr. Krabs advice to heart. Luckily&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.400 --> 00:08:30.000
for Sam though, he only had to spend 10 days in&nbsp;
jail until he would make bail for a whopping $250&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:35.800
million which is thought to be the largest bail&nbsp;
in history. If this was just 2 months earlier,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.800 --> 00:08:41.840
Sam being able to afford $250 million for bail by&nbsp;
himself wouldn’t have been all that surprising,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:41.840 --> 00:08:47.240
but with FTX being bankrupt, you’re probably&nbsp;
wondering how he secured the bond. Well,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.240 --> 00:08:52.080
a part of the answer is that he got help from&nbsp;
his parents who put up their Palo Alto home as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:52.080 --> 00:08:57.840
collateral. Speaking of their Palo Alto home,&nbsp;
after making bail, Sam would be put under house&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.840 --> 00:09:02.880
arrest at his parent’s home. Since then, Sam&nbsp;
has mostly flied under the radar only talking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.880 --> 00:09:08.000
to the press behind the scenes but he has made&nbsp;
some notable appearances on headlines here and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.000 --> 00:09:15.880
there the first of which was on Jan 3, 2023 when&nbsp;
he pled not guilty as expected. Something that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:15.880 --> 00:09:20.840
not expected though was his headline appearance&nbsp;
in mid February when a judge ordered him to stop&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.840 --> 00:09:27.000
using VPNs to access the internet. According to&nbsp;
his lawyers, Sam was just using a VPN to watch&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:27.000 --> 00:09:31.680
the Super Bowl, but it’s very possible that he&nbsp;
could have been doing something to influence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.680 --> 00:09:36.840
the case in his favor which would not be all&nbsp;
that surprising given that he would do exactly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.840 --> 00:09:43.920
that just 6 months later. It August of 2023,&nbsp;
it came out that Sam may have been tampering&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.920 --> 00:09:49.160
with the witnesses. According to prosectuors, Sam&nbsp;
had shared documents with the press that made it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:49.160 --> 00:09:54.440
seem that Caroline was just a “jilted lover” who&nbsp;
had worked alone. This of course could not only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:54.440 --> 00:09:59.920
influence how Caroline testifies but may also&nbsp;
influence how other witnesses testiyfing. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.920 --> 00:10:15.187
as such, a federal judge would revoke Sam’s bail&nbsp;
putting him back behind bars on August 11, 2023.

00:10:15.187 --> 00:10:16.360
TRIAL BEGINS:
Sam’s lawyers have tried&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.360 --> 00:10:22.080
to argue that Sam being in jail is preventing him&nbsp;
from being able to prepare for trial, but a judge&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:22.080 --> 00:10:26.920
knocked this argument down saying that Sam has&nbsp;
already had several months and extensive access&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.920 --> 00:10:32.440
to electronics resources to prepare. And all of&nbsp;
this leads us into Sam’s trial which officially&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.440 --> 00:10:39.160
began on October 3, 2023 with jury selection.&nbsp;
The criminal trial is expected to last up to six&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.160 --> 00:10:44.560
weeks meaning that most of the case should play&nbsp;
out by mid November, but that doesn’t mean that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:49.040
we’ll get a verdict right after. Jury delibrations&nbsp;
can take anywhere from a few few minutes to even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.040 --> 00:10:56.440
a few months. Also, Sam may be up for a second&nbsp;
trial next March but we don’t know if that trial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:56.440 --> 00:11:02.640
will ever see the light of day. So, chances are&nbsp;
that unless we see some sort of crazy roadblock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:08.480
we should have a verdict by the end of this&nbsp;
year. What will that verdict be? Well, Sam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:08.480 --> 00:11:14.040
is currently facing these 7 charges which carry&nbsp;
more than 100 years worth of prison time. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:19.600
even if he does convicted on all these charges,&nbsp;
it’s almost certain that Sam won’t be spending&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.600 --> 00:11:24.760
anywhere near a 100 years in prison. According&nbsp;
to legal experts, similiar charges are usually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:28.480
grouped meaning that prison time is will be&nbsp;
assigned in a concurrent manner as opposed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.480 --> 00:11:34.160
a consecutive manner. As such, if Sam does end&nbsp;
up getting convicted, his total prison sentence&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.160 --> 00:11:39.760
will probably be in the 10 to 20 year range. It’s&nbsp;
unlikely, however, that he would actually end up&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:39.760 --> 00:11:45.280
spending all of that time in prison. For starters,&nbsp;
if the case isn’t going his way and his lawyers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:45.280 --> 00:11:50.360
feel that he’s probably gonna be found guilty,&nbsp;
it’s very possible that they negotiate a plea deal&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:50.360 --> 00:11:56.080
with the prosecutors which could bring his prison&nbsp;
time to less than 10 years. Also, if he is guilty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:56.080 --> 00:12:00.760
he would likely have a lot of dirt on the people&nbsp;
around him that made all of this possible meaning&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:00.760 --> 00:12:05.280
that he could cash in his knowledge for an&nbsp;
even shorter sentence. This is exactly what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.280 --> 00:12:09.920
the Wolf of Wall Street did. Originally,&nbsp;
he was sentenced for 4 years in prison,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:16.240
but by turning on his associates, he was able to&nbsp;
get out after 22 months. With that being said,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:16.240 --> 00:12:20.920
it’s highly unlikely that Sam gets away with no&nbsp;
prison time either just due to the scale of his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.920 --> 00:12:27.000
disaster. So, the most likely outcome is probably&nbsp;
some amount of prison time but not for the rest of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:27.000 --> 00:12:33.840
his life which if he is guilty, you could argue he&nbsp;
deserves. With over $8 billion worth of customer&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:33.840 --> 00:12:39.440
funds missing according to the CFTC, it’s no&nbsp;
question that Sam has completely ruined the lives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:39.440 --> 00:12:44.480
of countless people. We’re talking about people&nbsp;
who lost their entire life savings, college funds,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:51.560
retirement funds, and house funds. And according&nbsp;
to Sam himself, this money is invaluable. In fact,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:51.560 --> 00:12:57.120
in a post he made about altruism before all&nbsp;
of this, he posed the question “Just how much&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:57.120 --> 00:13:04.120
impact can a dollar have?” His answer was 1/2000&nbsp;
of a life. So, if Sam really is the reason that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.120 --> 00:13:12.120
people lost over $8 billion, then Sam’s “mistake”&nbsp;
by his own words are worth four million lives. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.120 --> 00:13:16.880
whether he spends rest of his life in jail&nbsp;
or not, this damage is something that he’ll&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.880 --> 00:13:23.520
likely never be able to make up for, and that’s&nbsp;
what happened to Sam Bankman-Fried. Someone else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:23.520 --> 00:13:28.760
that’s facing legal consequences is Amazon who may&nbsp;
be broken up. Check out this video to learn more.

